 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "What it does",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "A low-frequency oscillator and divider count out the interval chosen by REXT. At the start of each interval WAKE goes high for 20 ms to rouse the microcontroller, which must answer with a rising edge on DONE. Missing that answer brings RSTn low; the DONE, WAKE and RSTn trio is the watchdog."
  },
  {
   "topic": "WAKE",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "WAKE rests low and pulses when an interval begins. No pulse is sent at the start of the very first interval, nor at the start of an interval that follows one in which DONE never came. If DONE arrives while WAKE is still high, WAKE drops as soon as DONE is seen."
  },
  {
   "topic": "DONE",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "A low-to-high edge counts as DONE; it must be at least 100 ns wide. Only the first DONE in an interval is acted on, and it clears only the watchdog counter. DONE has to arrive 20 ms or more ahead of the following WAKE rising edge (tDDONE max = tIP - 20 ms)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "RSTn",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "RSTn is open drain; a pull-up is required, and 100 kΩ is the value given to keep current low. It is held low through power-on reset and the REXT measurement. Afterwards it goes low for tRSTn (320 ms) when DELAY/M_RST stays high for two internal oscillator cycles (about 20 ms), or when an interval starts without DONE having arrived 20 ms before the WAKE edge."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Start-up",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "After power-on reset the part measures the resistance on DELAY/M_RST once, sending a constant current into REXT for tR_EXT (100 ms); supply current is 200 µA typ during this. RSTn is released tR_EXT + tRSTn after power-up, and the first WAKE comes a further tIP later."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Normal operation",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "WAKE pulses repeat every tIP while DONE keeps arriving. A manual reset, or a late or missing DONE, asserts RSTn and restarts the internal counters. Reset-to-reset or reset-to-WAKE spacing is tIP + tRSTn."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Setting the interval",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "tIP is the time between two WAKE rising edges, chosen by REXT from DELAY/M_RST to ground in the range 500 Ω to 170 kΩ; a 1% resistor or better is recommended. The value is read only once, after power-up. The 1650 settings cover 100 ms to 7200 s: the first nine are 100 ms steps up to 900 ms and the other 1641 span 1 s to 7200 s. Each is discrete, so a wanted interval carries a quantization error."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Manual reset",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "Tying DELAY/M_RST to VDD is a manual reset; it counts as valid when held for 20 ms or longer within a 30 ms observation window, and the pin threshold is 1.5 V. A reset during power-on or the resistance reading aborts the reading, which restarts when the switch is let go. A valid reset clears every counter, sets WAKE low and RSTn low; RSTn falls tM_RST after the reset begins and stays low until the reset ends plus tDB plus tRSTn, give or take about 5 ms. The counters resume once the pin is released and tRSTn has passed."
  },
  {
   "topic": "DELAY/M_RST pin",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "During power-on and the resistance reading the pin connects through a multiplexer to an analog measuring chain; afterwards that chain is powered down and the pin feeds digital logic with a debounce filter that rejects glitches."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Manual-reset switch choice",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "An SPST switch from VDD to DELAY/M_RST, with REXT left in place, is the cheaper choice but draws VDD/REXT while pressed. An SPDT switch that moves the pin from REXT to VDD draws no current during reset."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Resistor tolerance and interval accuracy",
   "page": 13,
   "text": "A computed REXT is often not a standard value; two standard parts in parallel come close, but their tolerance moves the interval. Find REXT_MIN and REXT_MAX from the chosen parts and tolerances, turn each into an interval with the TADC equation, then into an error with the quantization-error equation. Worked case: 600 s needs 57.44 kΩ, made from 107 kΩ and 124 kΩ at 1%. Their parallel uncertainty is 0.82%, so REXT lies between 56.96 kΩ and 57.90 kΩ, the intervals between 586.85 s and 611.3 s, and the error between -1.88% and +2.19%. The spread is lopsided because the resistance-to-time conversion is quadratic."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Data-logger design",
   "page": 15,
   "text": "In a battery data logger the sensor front end and microcontroller idle most of the time. With the timer handling timing and watchdog, the microcontroller can sit in its deepest sleep mode; the interval is picked after a power budget of every device in each of its modes, to stretch battery life."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Power supply",
   "page": 17,
   "text": "Supply 1.8 V to 5.5 V, with a 0.1 µF X7R multilayer ceramic capacitor recommended between VDD and GND."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Layout",
   "page": 17,
   "text": "Stray capacitance on DELAY/M_RST can disturb the interval measurement, so keep the REXT connection from that pin to ground as short as possible. Short WAKE and RSTn traces to the microcontroller also keep their signals clean."
  }
 ],
